    "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel"},
    "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel", "url": "https://fileintel.me"},
    "description": "Removing metadata from a signed PDF usually invalidates the signature. Here's the byte-range math behind why, and the three workflows that actually work.",
    "url": "https://fileintel.me/blog/remove-metadata-from-signed-pdf",
    "mainEntityOfPage": "https://fileintel.me/blog/remove-metadata-from-signed-pdf"
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I remove just the Author field from a signed PDF without breaking the signature?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No, not if the Author field existed in the document at the moment it was signed. The signature hash covers the entire byte range of the file as it existed at signing time, including the Info dictionary. Deleting or editing even one field inside that range changes the hash and the signature will validate as broken, regardless of how small the edit is."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does Adobe Acrobat's Remove Hidden Information tool preserve signatures?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Acrobat's Remove Hidden Information and Sanitize Document tools rewrite the underlying file structure, which invalidates any existing digital signature. Acrobat will typically warn you before running the tool on a signed file, and the resulting document shows as unsigned or signature-invalid afterward."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What happens if I flatten a signed PDF to remove metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Flattening rewrites the PDF's internal object structure to bake form fields, annotations, and sometimes the visual signature stamp into the page content. This process always breaks the cryptographic signature because it changes bytes across the entire file, not just the metadata fields. What remains after flattening is a document that looks signed but is not cryptographically verifiable."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is XMP metadata covered by the signature the same way as the Info dictionary?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Whatever exists inside the signed byte range at the time of signing is covered, and that includes the XMP metadata stream if it was present. Some signing tools even write additional XMP fields as part of the signing process itself, meaning both metadata layers are locked in together."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I add a second signature after removing metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "You can add a second signature to a document, but that does not restore validity to the first one if you altered the file in between. Each signature only vouches for the state of the file at the moment it was applied. If the first signer's signature shows invalid, a second signature does not fix that — it only confirms the second signer approved the file in its post-edit state."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Will courts accept a PDF whose signature shows invalid due to metadata removal?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "This depends entirely on the court, the jurisdiction, and whether cryptographic validity is required for admissibility versus a visual signature block being sufficient. Many e-filing systems specifically check signature validity and will reject a submission that fails validation. When in doubt, treat the originally signed file as the authoritative record and never modify it after execution."}
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    "name": "How to handle metadata on a PDF that needs a digital signature",
    "totalTime": "PT5M",
    "step": [
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 1, "name": "Strip metadata before routing for signature", "text": "Run the document through a metadata remover before it is sent out for e-signature, so the Info dictionary and XMP stream are already clean when the signature hash is calculated."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 2, "name": "Verify the fields are actually gone", "text": "Reopen the document properties or run an inspection tool to confirm Author, Creator, Producer, and XMP fields are empty before sending the file to the signer."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 3, "name": "Sign the cleaned file", "text": "Route the already-cleaned document through your e-signature workflow. The resulting signed file will have no leftover metadata to strip later."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 4, "name": "If the file is already signed, treat edits as archival-only", "text": "For a document that is already signed, understand that any metadata removal will invalidate that signature. If you must produce a clean copy, keep the original signed file as your legal record and clearly label the stripped copy as a non-authoritative reference version."}
    ]
  }
  </script>

